<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Joan of Arc</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1412–1431</span> · Also known as Jeanne d'Arc, Maid of Orléans</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Joan of Arc was a peasant girl from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/domremy-la-pucelle">Domrémy</a>, on the borders of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lorraine">Lorraine</a> and Champagne, who believed that saints' voices called her to save <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hundred-years-war">Hundred Years' War</a>. In 1429 she helped lift the English siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orleans">Orléans</a> and brought Charles VII to his coronation at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reims">Reims</a>. Captured by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundians">Burgundians</a>, she was condemned for heresy by a pro-English church court and burned at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rouen">Rouen</a> in 1431; she was canonised in 1920.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/joan-of-arc.jpg" alt="Joan of Arc" width="320" height="477" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Joan_of_Arc_miniature_graded.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
